n process things up to one hour  on cloud run  next once again we are delivering  something that a lot of you  have been asking for um today  as i mentioned clodran scales autoscales  and notably scales down to zero so  that's great but that means that when  there is no traffic and suddenly there  is traffic that comes in  you have what we call a call start which  is the time it takes to  to start to go from zero container  instances to one container instances or  more  and this usually depends a lot on what  your container  is doing at startup time  with mean instances you can say hey i  want  a minimum of for example two instances  always one at any point in time and when  that's the case cloud1 will make sure  that you  you your num your number of instances  never goes below  this this specific the number you  specified  so that's great for improving  performances notably uh  when it scales down to zero but the good  news is that those instances  when they are not in used actually are  cheaper  than active instances another thing  we've added  um to help you better handled handle the  auto scaling of clutter  is that we are now sending a signal  before the instance is shutting down  so that gives you the time for example  to close any open database connection  or to send any data that you might not  have sent and kept in memory  by sending the sick term signal you have  up to 10 seconds of processing time  when you can do something before the  instance is shut down  we've also heard you we added support  for grpc so now out of the box  your cloud run service can expose a grpc  server  that you know nothing to configure just  your code has to start a grpc server  that listens on the same port as the one  we are sending  regular http requests so grpc on a  serverless infrastructure  yes that's possible with cloudrun  and lastly we've also heard your  feedback  we added support for server side  streaming  so what that means is that you can now  stream your responses  back to the clients you are not limited  anymore by the response size of  32 megabytes the responses are not  buffered anymore  that allows you to stream http and grpc  responses  in the example on the right what i'm  doing is my small web server is actually  serving a video file  to the client and i'm using this special  header transfer encoding  chunked that means that the client will  be downloading um  the stream of the bytes of this video  so hopefully uh this is removing some of  the limits  that were initially part of clauden and  don't worry we are not stopping here  we are we are keeping removing those  existing limits  so hopefully with that with this talk um  i convinced you  how uh claudine is now even more than  ever  enterprise ready and even more than ever  developer friendly and by removing some  of  its original limits gives you more  flexibility  we've been very hard at work to deliver  all of these features  that last year and we are of course  not going to stop here i want to thank  you for  um attending this session and we can't  wait to see  the great things you will build with  cloudrun  you
 

